    "body": "Beam Therapeutics’ risto-cel provided at least 12 consecutive months of freedom from severe pain crises for patients with sickle cell disease in a mid-stage trial, setting the stage for an FDA application as early as this year.\nBeam Therapeutics’ cell therapy risto-cel freed patients with sickle cell disease from severe pain crises for at least one year, teeing up what could be the first application for a base-editing therapy, possibly by the end of this year.\n“Positive pivotal results strengthen the risto-cel regulatory case and mark an important milestone for base editing,” H.C. Wainwright said in a Tuesday morning investor note.\nRistoglogene autogetemcel—dubbed risto-cel—met the Phase 1/2 BEACON trial ’s primary endpoint, Beam said on Tuesday, providing patients with sickle cell disease (SCD) with at least 12 consecutive months of freedom from severe vaso-occlusive crises (VOC)—or episodes of intense pain related to blocked vessels— based on independently adjudicated results. VOCs are a hallmark of SCD. As of Oct. 20, 39 adults and 10 adolescents had been treated with risto-cel, with 31 having completed the required follow-up period for the endpoint.\nBEACON also succeeded on all secondary endpoints, which included freedom from hospitalization for severe VOCs for at least 12 consecutive months.\nRisto-cel also met all of the trial’s primary safety endpoints at the interim analysis. The therapy’s profile was consistent with previously reported data from BEACON and with the known effects of the stem cell transplantation process.\nIn November 2024, Beam disclosed the death of a patient in BEACON, which according to the biotech occurred due to respiratory failure four months after receiving treatment. It was deemed unrelated to risto-cel and was instead “likely” linked to the busulfan-based chemotherapy immunosuppression regimen used in the stem cell transplant process.\nThe new Phase 1/2 readout will support an upcoming biologics license application (BLA), according to Beam. “With these results in hand, we are focused on submitting our planned BLA as early as year-end, which has the potential to be the first BLA for a base editing therapy,” Beam CEO John Evans said in a prepared statement.\nWhile the FDA has approved gene-edited therapies from CRISPR Therapeutics and Genetix Biotherapeutics, Beam will likely be the first to seek marketing approval for a therapy that uses base editing. Also working on base editing is Eli Lilly, after the acquisition of Verve Therapeutics for up to $1.3 billion in 2025.\nMergers & acquisitions\nLilly Validates Gene Editing Space With $1.3B Verve Buy, But Analysts Are Skeptical\nThe pending deal was rumored overnight after a report from the Financial Times , spurring analysts to speculate that if true, the entire gene editing space would see a boost at the markets.\nJune 17, 2025\n·\n3 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more\nThe biotech plans to present detailed results from BEACON at a medical meeting in 2027. BEAM will also present data from an earlier cutoff date at the American Society of Hematology (ASH) meeting in New Orleans next month.\nH.C. Wainwright noted that Beam did not disclose safety details nor the proportion of patients who achieved the primary efficacy endpoint.\n“The topline data establishes endpoint success, while detailed safety, response rates and longer-term outcomes remain the next focus,” the firm said.\nRisto-cel , formerly known as BEAM-101, is an ex vivo, autologous therapy comprising autologous CD34+ hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs) that have been base edited. SCD happens when a mutation in the beta-globin gene causes a mutated form of adult hemoglobin to collect into long, rigid molecules that bend red blood cells into a sickle shape, according to Beam. The edit in risto-cel is meant to inhibit the BCL11A gene from blocking expression of fetal hemoglobin, thereby increasing production of the non-sickling and anti-sickling version of the protein.\nAt ASH, H.C. Wainwright will be looking for fetal hemoglobin (HbF) induction of over 60% and reduction of sickle hemoglobin to lower than 40%—\"a ‘60/40’ trait that is viewed by physicians and regulators as a key goal in the treatment of SCD.”\nLast month, analysts named Beam as one potential beneficiary of the FDA’s new plug-and-play guidance, under which gene-editing therapies addressing various disease-causing mutations could be covered under one BLA.\nThe new draft guidance “converts the individualized ‘product variant’ concept into an operational licensing framework, with base editing the template modality,” H.C. Wainwright said in a note to investors late last month. The analysts specifically pointed to BEAM-304, currently in a Phase 1/2 trial for phenylketonuria (PKU). BEAM-304 is designed to correct mutations in the phenylalanine hydroxylase gene that cause the disease. In North America and Europe, there are thought to be four specific mutations that cause PKU.\nFDA\nPlug-and-play gene therapy: FDA executes on vision that’s been in the works for years\nFormer biologics boss Peter Marks had a vision. With the FDA’s latest draft guidance, it’s coming to fruition. Speaking with BioSpace, current CBER leader Karim Mikhail said the “adapted pathway” is designed to develop personalized gene therapies more quickly.\nNovember 2, 2026\n·\n6 min read\n·\nHeather McKenzie\nRead more",
